window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-798fa662679f3e1a"]={"key":"ny_main-798fa662679f3e1a","group":"ny_main","uid":"798fa662679f3e1a","rank":91,"title":"clinical information follow up training revision","occupation":"nurse_practitioners","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You are Sofia Adeyemi, a Nurse Practitioner at Ballantry Studios, and I need you to take ownership of a revised clinical-information training document for our operations and delivery colleagues. Ludmila Aubert returned the team’s draft during the week of 6 October 2025 after reviewers noticed that staff use “follow-up” to mean two different things: a scheduled clinical review and any later contact. The draft also leaves out the rare but serious situation in which deterioration is disclosed during an otherwise routine contact. Nikolai Baranowski has already promised Heronbank Foundry that we will provide a short, reassuring revision by 10 October 2025, and Karin Arriaga and Aurelio Arriaga will need to use the same document when the Heronbank Foundry compliance reviewer examines it. That promise is creating pressure to simplify the wording more than professional communication standards allow. We also have only a limited review window and no capacity for the full textbook treatment of every possible contact pathway, so the document needs a defensible, bounded fallback rather than pretending to be comprehensive. Please create one revised training document, supplied in both `follow_up_training_revision.docx` and `follow_up_training_revision.pdf`, and write both files into the top level of the working directory where you start, using exactly those filenames. The Word file and the PDF are two formats of the same document, not two different versions, and any dates, limits, labels, or other figures shown in one must agree with the other. Keep the document to no more than two pages so it can be reviewed and reused in operations. There are no source attachments: work from sound Nurse Practitioner communication practice, but do not invent a patient, symptoms, diagnosis, outcome, policy, or event beyond the situation described here. Make the intended audience clear for both the staff who need to understand the terminology and the Heronbank Foundry reviewer who needs to assess the revision. Please make the document usable without a follow-up question and suitable for practice in England and Wales as at 6 October 2025, while avoiding unsupported legal or clinical claims. Make the document cover these requirements in a coherent sequence: (1) state its purpose and scope; (2) acknowledge the ambiguity around “follow-up” and adopt one defensible terminology convention, explaining that convention plainly; (3) distinguish the scheduled clinical-review meaning from the broader meaning of later contact, using enough neutral examples that staff will not apply the chosen labels inconsistently; (4) present the exceptional deterioration disclosure as a clearly bounded case separate from ordinary guidance, without turning it into a fictional case study or adding clinical facts; (5) show what has materially changed between the earlier draft and this revision, including both the terminology problem and the previously absent exceptional case; (6) make the limits of the document visible so that the pressure for a simple promise does not become an unsupported assurance; and (7) finish with a compact staff-and-oversight summary that remains useful when read on its own. Do not write system click instructions, a treatment protocol, triage advice, or a recommendation about what action a clinician should take in the exceptional case; this is a focused training and oversight document that records distinctions and boundaries, not a substitute for patient-specific judgement. Use clear, respectful language, preserve the serious nature of the exception without sensationalising it, and ensure that the final files are readable, editable where appropriate, and visually consistent.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `follow_up_training_revision.docx` — The editable revised clinical-information training document for operations and delivery staff and oversight review. (Write to the top level of the solver's starting working directory.; Use exactly the specified filename.; No more than two pages.; Must be the same substantive document as the PDF.; Must not introduce unsupported patient, legal, policy, diagnosis, treatment, or outcome claims.)\n- `follow_up_training_revision.pdf` — A readable PDF rendition of the same revised clinical-information training document for Heronbank Foundry oversight review. (Write to the top level of the solver's starting working directory.; Use exactly the specified filename.; No more than two pages.; Must agree substantively with the DOCX, including dates, limits, terminology, and case description.; Must be readable without clipped, missing, or overlapping content.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/follow_up_training_revision.docx","bytes":40093,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/0c3b2200b10a8fbaf9b8f39bab761e25aba846c017ceec799a6f233cb16c58c1.docx","sha256":"0c3b2200b10a8fbaf9b8f39bab761e25aba846c017ceec799a6f233cb16c58c1","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"BALLANTRY STUDIOS   •   PRACTICE COMMUNICATION\nClinical-information training revision\nTerminology and boundaries for routine clinical-information contacts\nAUDIENCE\nOperations & delivery staff;\nHeronbank Foundry oversight reviewer\nSCOPE\nEngland and Wales\nas at 6 October 2025\nSTATUS\nRevised training information\nPrepared by Sofia Adeyemi, Nurse Practitioner\nPurpose and bounded scope\nThis revised clinical-information training document gives operations and delivery colleagues one clear way to describe routine clinical-information contacts. It also enables the Heronbank Foundry reviewer to see the material revision and its boundaries. It is focused training information: it is not patient-specific clinical guidance, a treatment protocol, a triage aid, or a complete contact pathway.\nThe terminology convention\nThe earlier draft used “follow-up” for distinct meanings: a scheduled clinical review and any later contact. That ambiguity can make otherwise routine communication difficult to compare. This revision reserves the term for the scheduled clinical-review meaning only.\nUSE THIS LABEL\nMEANING IN THIS REVISION\nScheduled clinical review\nA planned, identifiable clinical review. “Scheduled follow-up” may be used as its shortened form; do not use the standalone label “follow-up”.\nLater contact\nAny later interaction that is not being described as a scheduled clinical review. “Routine later contact” may be used where the contact is routine.\nApply the distinction\n• Scheduled clinical review: use this where the contact is planned and identifiable as a clinical review. The label describes that planned review relationship, not a frequency, indication or outcome.\n• Later contact: use this for a later interaction that was not arranged and identified as a scheduled clinical review, including a routine information contact or an unplanned communication.\n• When wording is unclear: do not infer that a later contact was a scheduled clinical review. Use “later contact” unless it is being described as the planned, identifiable review.\nBounded exception — deterioration disclosed during an otherwise routine contact\nRare but serious. A disclosure of deterioration during an otherwise routine contact is separate from the ordinary terminology distinction. It must not be presented as merely a routine later contact for the sake of simple wording. This document records that communication boundary only: it does not describe the disclosure, diagnose it, predict an outcome, or direct a clinical response. The case sits outside this document’s ordinary guidance.\n\n\nREVISION RECORD & REUSABLE SUMMARY\nWhat changed — and what this short document does not claim\nMaterial changes from the earlier draft\nREVISION FOCUS\nEARLIER DRAFT\nTHIS REVISION / WHY IT MATTERS\nTerminology\nUsed “follow-up” for both a scheduled clinical review and any later contact.\nReserves the label for a scheduled clinical review and names the other category “later contact”. Staff can classify ordinary communications consistently; review can test one visible convention.\nExceptional boundary\nDid not state the rare but serious disclosure of deterioration during an otherwise routine contact.\nRecords it as a separate, bounded exception. The serious information is retained without turning this short training document into clinical guidance or a full pathway.\nLimits that remain visible\nThe revision is deliberately concise for a limited review window. Clear terms and a bounded exception are a useful fallback; they are not an assurance that every contact pathway, clinical question or documentation context is covered. No local policy, external requirement or clinical action is asserted here. The scope is communication and terminology in routine clinical-information contacts only.\nSTAFF & OVERSIGHT SUMMARY\nFor staff: call a planned, identifiable clinical review a “scheduled clinical review” (or “scheduled follow-up” only as its shortened form). Call another later interaction a “later contact”. Do not use the standalone label “follow-up”.\nFor oversight: verify that this convention is used consistently and that deterioration disclosed during an otherwise routine contact remains a distinct, serious boundary rather than ordinary terminology guidance.\nShared limit: this is focused training information for England and Wales as at 6 October 2025, not patient-specific guidance or a complete clinical pathway.\nReview use\nThis document is intended to be read on its own by operations and delivery colleagues and by the Heronbank Foundry reviewer. Its review value is the explicit convention, the visible exceptional boundary and the stated limits—not a promise of comprehensive coverage.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}},{"name":"package/private/gold/follow_up_training_revision.pdf","bytes":51129,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/14df94110e0b449b3da126a59a6b71b9c206266cc0c6238fee73687fa56eda8a.pdf","sha256":"14df94110e0b449b3da126a59a6b71b9c206266cc0c6238fee73687fa56eda8a","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":"BALLANTRY STUDIOS • PRACTICE COMMUNICATION\n\nClinical-information training revision\nTerminology and boundaries for routine clinical-information contacts\n\n   AUDIENCE                                                                        STATUS\n                                          SCOPE\n   Operations & delivery staff;                                                    Revised training information\n                                          England and Wales\n   Heronbank Foundry oversight                                                     Prepared by Sofia Adeyemi, Nurse\n                                          as at 6 October 2025\n   reviewer                                                                        Practitioner\n\nPurpose and bounded scope\nThis revised clinical-information training document gives operations and delivery colleagues one clear way to describe\nroutine clinical-information contacts. It also enables the Heronbank Foundry reviewer to see the material revision and its\nboundaries. It is focused training information: it is not patient-specific clinical guidance, a treatment protocol, a triage aid,\nor a complete contact pathway.\n\nThe terminology convention\nThe earlier draft used “follow-up” for distinct meanings: a scheduled clinical review and any later contact. That ambiguity\ncan make otherwise routine communication difficult to compare. This revision reserves the term for the scheduled\nclinical-review meaning only.\n   USE THIS LABEL                 MEANING IN THIS REVISION\n\n   Scheduled clinical\n   review\n   A planned, identifiable\n                                  Later contact\n   clinical review. “Scheduled\n                                  Any later interaction that is not being described as a scheduled clinical review. “Routine\n   follow-up” may be used as\n                                  later contact” may be used where the contact is routine.\n   its shortened form; do not\n   use the standalone label\n   “follow-up”.\n\nApply the distinction\n• Scheduled clinical review: use this where the contact is planned and identifiable as a clinical review. The label\n  describes that planned review relationship, not a frequency, indication or outcome.\n• Later contact: use this for a later interaction that was not arranged and identified as a scheduled clinical review,\n  including a routine information contact or an unplanned communication.\n• When wording is unclear: do not infer that a later contact was a scheduled clinical review. Use “later contact”\n  unless it is being described as the planned, identifiable review.\n\nBounded exception — deterioration disclosed during an otherwise routine contact\n Rare but serious. A disclosure of deterioration during an otherwise routine contact is separate from the ordinary\n terminology distinction. It must not be presented as merely a routine later contact for the sake of simple wording. This\n document records that communication boundary only: it does not describe the disclosure, diagnose it, predict an outcome, or\n direct a clinical response. The case sits outside this document’s ordinary guidance.\n\n\n\n\n                       BALLANTRY STUDIOS | CLINICAL-INFORMATION TRAINING REVISION | ENGLAND AND WALES\n\fREVISION RECORD & REUSABLE SUMMARY\n\nWhat changed — and what this short document does\nnot claim\nMaterial changes from the earlier draft\n  REVISION FOCUS            EARLIER DRAFT                                      THIS REVISION / WHY IT MATTERS\n\n                                                                               Reserves the label for a scheduled clinical\n                                                                               review and names the other category “later\n                            Used “follow-up” for both a scheduled clinical\n  Terminology                                                                  contact”. Staff can classify ordinary\n                            review and any later contact.\n                                                                               communications consistently; review can test\n                                                                               one visible convention.\n\n                                                                               Records it as a separate, bounded exception.\n                            Did not state the rare but serious disclosure of\n                                                                               The serious information is retained without\n  Exceptional boundary      deterioration during an otherwise routine\n                                                                               turning this short training document into\n                            contact.\n                                                                               clinical guidance or a full pathway.\n\nLimits that remain visible\nThe revision is deliberately concise for a limited review window. Clear terms and a bounded exception are a useful\nfallback; they are not an assurance that every contact pathway, clinical question or documentation context is covered. No\nlocal policy, external requirement or clinical action is asserted here. The scope is communication and terminology in\nroutine clinical-information contacts only.\n STAFF & OVERSIGHT SUMMARY\n For staff: call a planned, identifiable clinical review a “scheduled clinical review” (or “scheduled follow-up” only as its\n shortened form). Call another later interaction a “later contact”. Do not use the standalone label “follow-up”.\n For oversight: verify that this convention is used consistently and that deterioration disclosed during an otherwise\n routine contact remains a distinct, serious boundary rather than ordinary terminology guidance.\n Shared limit: this is focused training information for England and Wales as at 6 October 2025, not patient-specific\n guidance or a complete clinical pathway.\n\nReview use\nThis document is intended to be read on its own by operations and delivery colleagues and by the Heronbank Foundry\nreviewer. Its review value is the explicit convention, the visible exceptional boundary and the stated limits—not a promise\nof comprehensive coverage.\n\n\n\n\n                       BALLANTRY STUDIOS | CLINICAL-INFORMATION TRAINING REVISION | ENGLAND AND WALES\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The editable deliverable exists as follow_up_training_revision.docx in the top level of the working directory.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Inspect the submitted files and their paths.","grader_hint":"Award only if the exact filename and extension are present at the required top-level location."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The review deliverable exists as follow_up_training_revision.pdf in the top level of the working directory.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Inspect the submitted files and their paths.","grader_hint":"Award only if the exact filename and extension are present at the required top-level location."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The DOCX document is no more than two pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Render or paginate the DOCX and count its pages.","grader_hint":"Use the rendered document, not an estimate based on source word count; two pages is compliant."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The PDF document is no more than two pages and has no clipped or overlapping content.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Inspect PDF page count and rendered page images.","grader_hint":"A two-page PDF is acceptable only when text and any tables remain legible and complete."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The DOCX and PDF communicate the same substantive training content, with no contradictory terminology, dates, limits, or case description.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"Compare extracted text and rendered content across both files.","grader_hint":"Minor pagination or formatting differences do not matter; omissions, additions, or contradictions in meaning do."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies itself as a revised clinical-information training document and states a clear purpose.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Read the title or opening statement and purpose language.","grader_hint":"The purpose must concern consistent communication and terminology, not merely say that the document is a revision."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies both its operational staff audience and its oversight or compliance-review audience.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Inspect audience, scope, and summary language.","grader_hint":"It should be evident what staff need to take from the document and what a reviewer is being asked to verify."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document confines its scope to communication and terminology in routine clinical-information contacts rather than presenting a complete clinical pathway.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Read the scope and boundary statements.","grader_hint":"Award when the scope is bounded and practical; do not require a particular heading."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states that its jurisdictional scope is England and Wales and gives 6 October 2025 as the relevant as-of date.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Search the document for the jurisdiction and date.","grader_hint":"Both the named jurisdiction and the as-of date must be present and unambiguous."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document explicitly identifies that “follow-up” is being used for two different kinds of later interaction.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Inspect the terminology discussion.","grader_hint":"The two meanings must be distinguishable as a scheduled clinical review and a broader later contact, even if the document uses different labels."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document deliberately adopts one coherent terminology convention and makes the choice understandable to its readers.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Read the definition and application of the chosen convention.","grader_hint":"Either reserving “follow-up” for scheduled review or retaining it as an umbrella term with explicit modifiers is acceptable; silently mixing the two is not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The scheduled clinical-review meaning is described as planned, identifiable, and distinct from an unplanned later interaction.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Inspect definitions and examples.","grader_hint":"The description should convey the planned review relationship without inventing a frequency, interval, or clinical indication."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The broader category of any later contact is described separately from the scheduled clinical-review category.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Inspect the terminology comparison.","grader_hint":"Later contact may include an interaction not arranged as a scheduled review, but the document must not collapse that distinction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document supplies neutral, realistic examples or a comparison device that lets staff classify the two ordinary meanings consistently.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Review examples, table, or equivalent comparison.","grader_hint":"Examples should clarify category boundaries and should not introduce patient-specific facts or a new local coding system."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The chosen terminology is applied consistently in the document’s definitions, examples, revision summary, and final summary.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Trace the terms through all substantive sections.","grader_hint":"A reader should not have to infer whether the same word changes meaning from one section to another."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document explicitly includes the rare but serious disclosure of deterioration during an otherwise routine contact.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Search for explicit language about deterioration disclosed during routine contact.","grader_hint":"The exception must be visible in the text, not merely implied by a generic statement about unusual contacts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deterioration disclosure is presented as a bounded exception separate from the ordinary terminology pathway.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Inspect how the exceptional case is labelled and connected to the ordinary guidance.","grader_hint":"The document should show why the exception cannot be treated as routine later contact without prescribing a clinical response."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The serious nature of the exceptional case is communicated clearly and proportionately, without sensational language.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Read the exception section as an operations reader and reviewer.","grader_hint":"The exception should feel materially important but not dramatic, speculative, or alarmist."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not invent a patient identity, symptoms, diagnosis, treatment, outcome, contact date, or event detail beyond the supplied situation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Check all narrative examples and exception wording against the request.","grader_hint":"Generic examples are acceptable when clearly generic; fabricated case facts are not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not turn the exceptional disclosure into a diagnosis, prognosis, triage rule, or treatment recommendation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Inspect clinical claims in the exception and boundary sections.","grader_hint":"Award for disciplined description of the communication distinction, not for adding medical content."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document records the exceptional case without directing the clinician or staff member to take a particular clinical action.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Search for imperative action, escalation, referral, emergency, or treatment instructions.","grader_hint":"A statement that the case falls outside ordinary guidance is acceptable; an action algorithm is not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document avoids unsupported claims that a named law, regulator, policy, insurer, or professional body requires a particular wording or response.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Inspect citations, authority claims, and mandatory-language assertions.","grader_hint":"The jurisdictional scope may be stated, but specific external obligations must not be invented."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document makes its limitations visible, including that it is focused training information rather than patient-specific clinical guidance or a complete pathway.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Read the scope and closing boundary statements.","grader_hint":"The limitation should prevent the short format from being mistaken for comprehensive authority."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document compares the earlier draft with the revised version and identifies both the terminology ambiguity and the omission of the exceptional case as material changes.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Inspect the revision or change-summary content.","grader_hint":"A mere statement that the document was improved is insufficient; both material changes must be named."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The change comparison explains why the two revisions matter to consistent staff communication and oversight review.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Read the explanation accompanying the change summary.","grader_hint":"Look for practical significance, not a cosmetic list of edits."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document handles the pressure created by Nikolai Baranowski’s promise for a simple, reassuring revision without removing material qualification or offering an unsupported assurance.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Assess the balance of brevity, reassurance, seriousness, and limits across the document.","grader_hint":"The document need not mention the interpersonal situation by name, but it must visibly preserve the information a professional reviewer would consider material."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document is a defensible concise fallback under the limited review window: focused enough to fit the cap while still retaining both the terminology distinction and the exceptional case.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Assess scope, completeness, and page-limited usability together.","grader_hint":"Do not reward either an overlong pseudo-textbook or a short document that achieves brevity by omitting the difficult content."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A staff reader can navigate the document and understand the terminology boundaries without asking the author to interpret them.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Perform an independent usability read of the rendered artifact.","grader_hint":"Clear labels, hierarchy, concise prose, and a useful comparison are evidence; dense unexplained prose is not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"An oversight reviewer can identify the document’s purpose, scope, revision focus, and treatment of the exceptional case without reconstructing the author’s reasoning.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Review the artifact from the compliance-review perspective.","grader_hint":"The document should make its boundaries and material changes auditable at a glance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document remains focused on the stated ambiguity and exceptional disclosure rather than expanding into unrelated clinical education, system instructions, or a full operating procedure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Inspect the full document for scope creep.","grader_hint":"Award when the document uses the limited space for the requested communication problem."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document contains no unsupported numerical thresholds, frequencies, response times, or other invented figures beyond dates or limits supplied in the request.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Inspect every number and quantitative claim in both files.","grader_hint":"Generic wording is preferable to fabricated precision; the two-page cap and supplied dates are not defects."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any revision date, effective date, page-limit statement, or other numeric metadata used in the DOCX is identical in meaning to the corresponding metadata in the PDF.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Compare dates, numbers, labels, and metadata across both rendered deliverables.","grader_hint":"Award only when shared figures and dated claims agree across formats."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not use the unqualified word “follow-up” in a way that defeats the convention it adopted.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Search each use of “follow-up” and compare it with the stated convention.","grader_hint":"An umbrella convention may use the word with clear modifiers; an exclusive convention must not casually revert to the ambiguous usage."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The closing staff-and-oversight summary accurately preserves the selected terminology convention, the ordinary distinction, the exceptional boundary, and the document’s limits.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Read the final summary independently from the preceding sections.","grader_hint":"The ending should function as a reliable stand-alone recap rather than a new recommendation or an invitation to unspecified next steps."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is a bounded Nurse Practitioner communication and training deliverable for an operations team and an external compliance reviewer. The practitioner must make a terminology convention explicit, preserve a serious exception without inventing clinical facts, and produce a concise document under review-time pressure.","difficulty_sources":["Resolve the ambiguity in a routinely overloaded clinical communication term without making the scope unusably broad.","Preserve and clearly bound a serious exception while avoiding invented clinical facts or action recommendations.","Balance a promise of a simple short document against the need for professionally honest qualification.","Produce one coherent, review-ready document in two agreeing file formats under a strict page cap."],"authoring_plan":{"source_policy":"No reference files are supplied. The build agent should create the gold expectation from stable Nurse Practitioner communication practice and the explicit scenario constraints, not from live research or invented local policy.","artifact_strategy":"The solver is expected to create one concise, normative training document and render it identically as DOCX and PDF. The reference solution should be a polished two-page maximum document that serves operations staff and an external oversight reviewer.","core_content_constraints":["The document must explicitly acknowledge that the term follow-up has been used for both a scheduled clinical review and any later contact.","Accept either of two deliberate conventions: reserve follow-up for scheduled clinical review and use another clearly explained term for later contact, or retain follow-up as an umbrella term while consistently qualifying the scheduled-review and later-contact meanings.","The convention must be explicit and applied consistently in definitions, examples, revision summary, and closing summary.","The scheduled clinical-review category must be described as planned and distinct from an unplanned or otherwise later contact, without inventing frequency, interval, indication, or patient facts.","The exceptional case must explicitly describe deterioration disclosed during a routine contact and must visibly separate that case from ordinary terminology guidance.","The exceptional case must not add symptoms, diagnosis, prognosis, treatment, outcome, urgency threshold, escalation route, or other clinical facts not supplied.","The document must not recommend an action for the exceptional case; it should record the communication boundary and why the case is outside ordinary guidance.","The revision comparison must identify the two material changes: resolving the ambiguous terminology and adding the previously absent exceptional case.","The document should acknowledge the bounded nature of the deliverable so the short-format promise does not become an unsupported assurance or imply comprehensive coverage.","The jurisdictional scope should be England and Wales as at 6 October 2025, but the document should avoid invented legal, regulatory, insurer, or professional-body requirements.","The document should be usable by operations staff without author clarification and reviewable by Heronbank Foundry oversight staff.","No unsupported quantitative thresholds or made-up case details should appear."],"acceptable_variation":"Titles, section labels, typography, examples, and wording may vary. The two terminology conventions described above are both acceptable if the solver chooses one deliberately and uses it consistently. Generic examples are acceptable only when they clarify classification without introducing a fictional patient or clinical event.","cross_deliverable_gold":"The DOCX and PDF gold outputs should contain the same substantive text and the same dates, limits, terminology labels, and case description. Pagination may differ only if both remain within the two-page cap and visually readable."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"primary_plus_appendix","structure":"required_sections"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"revised_training_docx","filename":"follow_up_training_revision.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"The editable revised clinical-information training document for operations and delivery staff and oversight review.","required":true,"constraints":["Write to the top level of the solver's starting working directory.","Use exactly the specified filename.","No more than two pages.","Must be the same substantive document as the PDF.","Must not introduce unsupported patient, legal, policy, diagnosis, treatment, or outcome claims."],"rendering_required":true},{"deliverable_id":"revised_training_pdf","filename":"follow_up_training_revision.pdf","media_type":"application/pdf","description":"A readable PDF rendition of the same revised clinical-information training document for Heronbank Foundry oversight review.","required":true,"constraints":["Write to the top level of the solver's starting working directory.","Use exactly the specified filename.","No more than two pages.","Must agree substantively with the DOCX, including dates, limits, terminology, and case description.","Must be readable without clipped, missing, or overlapping content."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"professional_report","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"professional_judgment"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"nurse_practitioners","sector":"healthcare_social_assistance","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":2.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"A domain-aware reviewer should assess the document for terminology discipline, clinical restraint, audience usability, handling of the exceptional disclosure, and agreement between the two formats. Automated checks may verify filenames, page counts, dates, and cross-file text, but professional judgement is required for scope and usability.","minimum_reviewers":1,"known_limitations":["The task intentionally supplies no local policy or clinical source pack, so the rubric assesses communication boundaries and restraint rather than jurisdiction-specific legal compliance.","Either of the two explicitly defensible terminology conventions is acceptable; grading must focus on deliberate choice and consistency."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T08:07:25+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T08:07:25+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T08:07:25+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T08:07:25+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/798fa662679f3e1a","declared_deliverables":["follow_up_training_revision.docx","follow_up_training_revision.pdf"]};
